## 4. Binary Arithmetic and Ripple-Carry Waves

Unary numbers take $n$ cells to represent $n$, so `add` takes $O(n)$ steps. Real hardware — and fast interaction nets — represent numbers in **binary**, using only $\lceil \log_2 n \rceil$ cells!

We represent a binary number **least-significant bit (LSB) first** using three Value cells:

- **`end`** — empty bit-string (`0`)
- **`0`** — append bit `0` (multiply by 2)
- **`1`** — append bit `1` (multiply by 2 and add 1)

So `5` (`101₂`) is `1 · 0 · 1 · end` and `3` (`011₂`) is `1 · 1 · end`.

First, look at **increment / carry (`+1`, `O_BINC`)**:
- **`+1 ⋈ end ⟶ 1 · end`** (allocate a new most-significant bit)
- **`+1 ⋈ 0 · x ⟶ 1 · x`** (carry absorbed in **1 step**!)
- **`+1 ⋈ 1 · x ⟶ 0 · (+1 ⋈ x)`** (flip `1` to `0` and ripple the carry one bit deeper!)

Binary addition (`+₂`, `O_BADD`) simply adds pairs of bits LSB-first and spawns a `+1` carry cell whenever `1 + 1 = 0 carry 1`. Watch `badd 5 3` (`101₂ + 011₂`) propagate a full ripple-carry wave across every bit to produce `1000₂ = 8` — then try `badd 7 1` or `badd 6 5`!

**[Binary Ripple-Carry Addition: `badd 5 3` →](/nets?preset=badd)**

